title The seventh park landed, and the red that booked the lap was mine
prompt the standing autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap in the amphora and bron-resins lane
rota lap 4250, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears: why the work exists. Threshold plus all three seats read. What it surfaced is the grain's own strand seated at the fusion, *a guard that cannot red guards nothing*, which is exactly what this lap found twice -- a plant standing in one `&&` chain with its own reading, and a link guard whose transform normalizes the byte that is wrong.
think Cold open scoped: 160 guards, 1414s, 155 green, 3 red, 2 gated. Reds first, so the constructive queue waited.
think Read all three off construction/standing-equipment-reds/ rather than guessing. shell_dialect red on gated_sed_i_sites=2 over a ceiling of 0; stash_record red on unlanded=2; standing_equipment red as the roll-up of both.
think The two sed -i sites were tools/am/amphora_mark_wreck_witness.rish lines 127 and 144 -- the witness I wrote last lap. My own red, one lap old, in my own lane.
think stash_record's unlanded=2 named two session logs in stash@{1}, and beside them the whole amphora_roster guard. path_absence_scan.sh answered absent here AND upstream for all four paths, so nothing had landed anywhere.
think Recovered by git checkout stash@{1} -- <path>, the TREE rather than the blob, so mode 100755 rode on the scan and the control. The exec-bit law's own distinction, applied.
think Re-applied the three shared-pin edits by act rather than by snapshot: the roster block at its seam, the amphora README paragraph, the fold recital line. A snapshot of a shared file is a rollback.
think The day count in two pins was derived rather than typed -- session-logs/README.md read 128 and CHAPTERS.md read 125 against a shelf holding 129.
obs THE RED THAT BOOKED THIS LAP WAS ONE LAP OLD AND MINE. sed -i is a GNU flag BSD sed reads as a backup suffix; shell_portable.sh's sed_inplace writes a temporary and cats it back through the original inode, which keeps the mode as well. Two sites, ceiling 0, guard back to 47/47.
obs AND SPLITTING THE PLANT FROM ITS READING FOUND A SECOND FAULT THE SAME SIZE. The elder leg ran its sed and its assertion in one && chain, so a sed that matched nothing stops the chain, the run exits non-zero, and the assert passes for the wrong reason -- REDS %519's shape, in a leg I wrote to prove a plant bites. Now the plant is applied and read back before the leg is aimed at it, and the new assert is proven to bite by planting a pattern that matches nothing.
obs SEVENTH PARK, AND THE FIRST WHERE THE PARKED WORK WAS ITSELF A RECOVERY. This lap's parked log records the sixth park recovering the fifth. %499 has now cost this ship four laps of recovery against one lap of production.
obs MY OWN CLOSED ROW'S FAULT WAS IN MY OWN COMMIT. Last lap closed *a rule is spelled twice where one reader cannot route through the other*, and its own fleet_call clause landed in .claude/rules/the-baton.md and never in the Cursor twin. rule_twin caught it at the cold open; I carried the clause over.
obs GRADING WHAT I TOUCHED FOUND THE ROOM'S REAL DAMAGE. The baton twin graded F on truth -- 24 of its sibling links name .md files that do not exist in .cursor/rules/. Across the room: 36 dangling links in 4 files, against 25 that already spell .mdc. rule_twin cannot see them, because its declared transform reads .mdc as .md -- it normalizes the very byte that is wrong. Repaired to zero; that twin reads B+ and the aroma twin A.
obs THE GATE'S OWN NUMBER IS AN ABSOLUTE OVER A GROWING POPULATION, and it reddened while its subject improved. Measured: 51 pairs, 11 agree, 40 drift, ceiling 36. The 40 pairs that existed when the ceiling was set now drift 35 -- one better. Eleven new pairs arrived and five of them drift, which is the whole of the rise. Three of those five are deliberate condensations no lap may reconcile without Keaton's word, and only 5 twins of 40 declare themselves condensed with a (full form) line -- so no instrument tells a short twin from a lapsed one. Named on the card rather than repaired, since the ceiling belongs to the ledger lane and reconciling is his word.
obs A WAIT ON A SHARED PIER HAS THE PKILL FAULT TOO. My first until-loop polled `pgrep -f standing_equipment_run.sh | head -1`, which on a pier eight ships share waits on whichever peer's runner is oldest rather than on mine. Re-armed on my own PID, read from /proc/<pid>/cwd. Same blind spot as %569, one verb over: a name-scoped reach is a fleet-scoped reach.
obs ELEVENTH COLLISION, AND THE CHEAPEST KIND. The rebase landed a peer's `%605` stamped 20260908.030037, six minutes ahead of my own booking at 030640. Mine was unshared, so it renumbered to `%606`: one `git mv` of the shelf and six one-line edits, because every living citation spelled the stamp `20260907.223214` rather than the number. reds_spine_derive reads rebindings=0 squatters=0 double_booked=0 verdict=ok after it. The row has now worn four numbers and one stamp.
obs THE REBASE CONFLICTED IN THREE SHARED PINS AND NONE OF MY OWN FILES. The card took upstream's GRASS block beside my own COPAL block; the REDS census line took upstream's whole, since it is generated and the pre-commit hook rewrites it at the amend; the fold recital kept both rows in stamp order. A snapshot of a shared file is a rollback, and an act re-applied to the current file is a merge.
